1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,431
안녕하세요!

2
00:00:00,431 --> 00:00:04,020
Today we'll learn Korean numbers, dates, and time.

3
00:00:04,020 --> 00:00:07,609
Just knowing numbers makes daily life much easier.

4
00:00:07,609 --> 00:00:08,470
Let's start!

5
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:17,500
Korean has two number systems: native 'goyueo' and Sino 한자어.

6
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:20,580
They're used differently, so learn both.

7
00:00:21,990 --> 00:00:29,880
First, native numbers: '하나', '둘', '셋', '넷', '다섯'.

8
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:33,100
Follow along slowly.

9
00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:40,499
Next, '여섯', '일곱', '여덟', '아홉', '열'.

10
00:00:40,499 --> 00:00:45,740
It helps to memorize up to ten.

11
00:00:47,630 --> 00:00:51,814
Native numbers count things and tell age.

12
00:00:51,814 --> 00:00:56,100
Like 사과 세 개 (three apples), 스무 살 (twenty).

13
00:00:56,550 --> 00:01:04,803
When counting, the shape changes: 하나 to '한 개',

14
00:01:04,803 --> 00:01:08,930
둘 to '두 개', 셋 to '세 개'.

15
00:01:11,210 --> 00:01:17,208
Age uses native too: '스무 살', '서른 살'.

16
00:01:17,208 --> 00:01:22,540
Just remember 스물 becomes '스무 살'.

17
00:01:23,490 --> 00:01:29,980
Now Sino numbers: '일', '이', '삼', '사', '오'.

18
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:34,770
Pronounce them short and clear.

19
00:01:36,730 --> 00:01:41,593
And '육', '칠', '팔', '구', '십'.

20
00:01:41,593 --> 00:01:47,150
Sino numbers are short and easy.

21
00:01:49,470 --> 00:01:57,189
Big numbers are Sino too: '십' is 10, '백' is 100,

22
00:01:57,189 --> 00:02:01,370
'천' is 1000, '만' is 10000.

23
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:13,737
Zero is read two ways: '영' or '공'.

24
00:02:13,737 --> 00:02:18,740
In phone numbers, we usually say '공'.

25
00:02:24,690 --> 00:02:29,990
Sino numbers are for dates, prices, phone numbers.

26
00:02:29,990 --> 00:02:31,580
Like 삼 월, 오천 원.

27
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:41,860
For prices, add '원' to Sino numbers.

28
00:02:41,860 --> 00:02:44,820
Say '삼천 원', '만 원'.

29
00:02:47,770 --> 00:02:52,994
Phone numbers are read digit by digit: '공일공... '.

30
00:02:52,994 --> 00:02:55,020
Remember, 0 is '공'.

31
00:02:59,190 --> 00:03:03,402
Time is fun: '시' (hour) uses native,

32
00:03:03,402 --> 00:03:07,730
'분' (minute) uses Sino. You mix them!

33
00:03:10,700 --> 00:03:15,505
So you say '세 시 삼십 분'.

34
00:03:15,505 --> 00:03:21,620
시 is native 세, 분 is Sino 삼십.

35
00:03:22,070 --> 00:03:29,390
A day splits into 오전 (AM) and 오후 (PM).

36
00:03:29,390 --> 00:03:36,710
Morning 오전, evening 오후. Like '오후 세 시'.

37
00:03:37,160 --> 00:03:45,227
Months add '월' to Sino: '일월', '이월'… up to '십이월',

38
00:03:45,227 --> 00:03:47,580
twelve months.

39
00:03:49,950 --> 00:03:56,078
The day's '일' is Sino too. '오늘은 며칠?

40
00:03:56,078 --> 00:03:58,880
' Answer '십오 일'.

41
00:04:00,980 --> 00:04:11,185
Days of the week: '월요일', '화요일', '수요일', '목요일',

42
00:04:11,185 --> 00:04:15,720
'금요일', '토요일', '일요일'.

43
00:04:16,770 --> 00:04:20,385
Recap! Counting and age use native; dates, time,

44
00:04:20,385 --> 00:04:22,870
prices use Sino; time mixes both.

45
00:04:29,820 --> 00:04:32,927
Today we learned numbers, dates, and time.

46
00:04:32,927 --> 00:04:36,108
Try saying today's date and time in Korean.

47
00:04:36,108 --> 00:04:37,070
다음 시간에 또 만나요!

